\fs72 2 - SELF CARE\
\

\fs24 Here are **50+ specific, surprising, embodied self-care practices**\'97tiny sensory rituals and unexpected kindnesses for yourself:\
\
---\
\
## **Temperature Play**\
\
1. **Warm Mug Hands** \'96 Wrap both hands around a warm (not hot) ceramic mug. Feel the heat seep into your palms, then spread up your forearms. Hold until the warmth matches your body temperature, then notice the cooling.\
\
2. **Cold Spoon Eyes** \'96 Keep two spoons in the freezer. When needed, press the curved backs gently against closed eyelids for 30 seconds. Feel the constriction, then the rush of warmth returning.\
\
3. **Hot Towel Face Wrap** \'96 Soak a washcloth in hot water, wring out, drape over your entire face covering eyes. Breathe the steam until it cools. Remove slowly, opening eyes into the humidity.\
\
4. **Ice Cube Trace** \'96 Run an ice cube slowly along your inner wrist, up to the elbow crease, down to the palm. Follow the melt water with your eyes.\
\
5. **Contrast Shower Ending** \'96 At the end of your shower, turn water to cool for 30 seconds, focusing on the back of your neck. Then warm again. Repeat once.\
\
6. **Warm Rice Sock** \'96 Fill a sock with rice, microwave 1-2 minutes. Place on belly, chest, or feet. Mold it to your contours like a weighted companion.\
\
7. **Cold Stone Hold** \'96 Keep a smooth stone in the fridge. Hold it when anxious, feeling the temperature neutralize with your body heat.\
\
---\
\
## **Textile & Touch**\
\
8. **Silk Scarf Drag** \'96 Run a silk scarf or smooth fabric slowly across your forearm, watching the skin goosebump in its wake. Reverse direction.\
\
9. **Velvet Rub** \'96 Find something velvet (pillow, clothing). Rub it between thumb and fingers, then press to your cheek. Notice the directional nap\'97smooth one way, resistant the other.\
\
10. **Loofa Slow Stroke** \'96 Use a dry loofa or brush to stroke your arm from shoulder to wrist, then back up. Count the bristle sensations.\
\
11. **Bare Foot Texture Tour** \'96 Walk slowly across different surfaces in your home: tile, wood, carpet, bathmat. Name the texture of each to yourself.\
\
12. **Weighted Blanket Corner** \'96 If you don't have a full weighted blanket, fold a heavy quilt into a square and place just on your chest or belly while lying down.\
\
13. **Fur/Plush Petting** \'96 If you have a pet, stroke slowly with the fur growth, then against. If not, use a plush toy or fuzzy blanket with the same attention.\
\
14. **Hand in Rice Bin** \'96 Fill a bowl with dry rice. Submerge your hand, close fist, release, letting grains fall through fingers. Listen to the sound.\
\
15. **Clay or Playdough Squeeze** \'96 Keep a small container near your desk. When stressed, squeeze until your hand tires, then slowly release finger by finger.\
\
---\
\
## **Scent & Memory**\
\
16. **Cotton Ball Perfume** \'96 Put a drop of essential oil or perfume on a cotton ball. Keep in a sealed bag. When needed, open and inhale deeply three times, then seal away to preserve the hit.\
\
17. **Coffee Bean Handful** \'96 Hold whole coffee beans in your palm. Crush one between fingers, inhale the fresh burst. Let the others warm in your hand.\
\
18. **Citrus Zest Under Nail** \'96 Zest a lemon or orange, then run your fingernail under the oily peel. Press that finger to your wrist pulse point. Smell throughout the day.\
\
19. **Old Book Smell** \'96 Find your oldest book. Open to the middle pages, bury your nose, inhale the vanilla-like scent of lignin decay.\
\
20. **Pencil Shavings** \'96 Sharpen a wooden pencil with a hand sharpener. Cup the shavings, inhale the cedar/graphite mix. Let it transport you to childhood classrooms.\
\
21. **Rain on Hot Asphalt Memory** \'96 If it's raining, step outside briefly. If not, wet a hot pan slightly and inhale the steam\'97petrichor, the smell of rain.\
\
---\
\
## **Sound & Vibration**\
\
22. **Tuning Fork Hum** \'96 If you have a tuning fork, strike it and press the base to your sternum. Feel the resonance in your chest cavity.\
\
23. **Glass Rim Sing** \'96 Wet your finger and run it around the rim of a wine glass until it sings. Hold the note, feel the vibration in your hand.\
\
24. **ASMR Brushing** \'96 Use a soft makeup brush on your own forearm, slowly, listening to the whisper sound. Or find a specific ASMR video of someone brushing velvet or hair.\
\
25. **White Noise Variation** \'96 Instead of generic rain, search for "brown noise" or "pink noise"\'97deeper, womb-like frequencies. Notice which your body prefers.\
\
26. **Bell or Chime Follow** \'96 Ring a small bell or chime. Listen until you can no longer hear it, even the memory of it. Count how long that takes.\
\
27. **Your Own Heartbeat** \'96 Lie on your side in a quiet room. Place your hand under your ear, pressing gently. Listen to your pulse until it becomes a rhythm you can sink into.\
\
28. **Humming Resonance** \'96 Hum a steady note. Place fingers on your cheekbones, then collarbone, then sternum. Feel where the vibration lives strongest.\
\
---\
\
## **Taste & Mouth**\
\
29. **Dark Chocolate Melt** \'96 Place a square on your tongue. Don't chew. Let it melt completely, noticing the stages: bitter, then creamy, then sweet release.\
\
30. **Honey Spoon** \'96 Take a teaspoon of raw honey. Let it sit on your tongue until it warms and thins. Swallow slowly, feeling it coat your throat.\
\
31. **Salt Crystal Crunch** \'96 Place a single flaky sea salt crystal on your tongue. Press it to the roof of your mouth. Let it dissolve grain by grain.\
\
32. **Cold Grape Slow Chew** \'96 Freeze grapes. Take one, let it warm slightly in your mouth, then bite slowly, feeling the ice crystals crack.\
\
33. **Warm Milk + Nutmeg** \'96 Heat milk (dairy or oat) with a grating of fresh nutmeg. Sip from a small cup, holding the warmth in your mouth before swallowing.\
\
34. **Herbal Tea Color Watch** \'96 Steep chamomile or hibiscus. Watch the color bloom in the water like ink. Don't stir. Let it happen on its own time.\
\
35. **Licorice Root Chew** \'96 Buy dried licorice root sticks. Chew the end until soft, sucking the sweet, earthy extract. Spit when spent.\
\
---\
\
## **Visual & Light**\
\
36. **Candle Flame Gaze** \'96 Light a candle in a dark room. Sit 3 feet away. Stare at the flame without blinking as long as possible. When you blink, close eyes and watch the afterimage.\
\
37. **Prism Rainbow Hunt** \'96 Hang a prism in a sunny window. Lie on the floor and let the moving rainbows fall across your face and body. Chase them with your hand.\
\
38. **Cloud Shape Watching** \'96 Lie on your back outside. Find one cloud. Watch it morph, naming the shapes until it dissolves entirely.\
\
39. **Star Twinkle Confirmation** \'96 Find a bright star. Stare at it until you see it twinkle (scintillation). Confirm to yourself: light is reaching my eye from light-years away.\
\
40. **Color Gradient Study** \'96 Find a wall or object with a gradient (sunset, ombre fabric). Stare at the transition point between two colors until the boundary blurs.\
\
41. **Eye Pillow Weight** \'96 Use a weighted eye pillow (or rice sock over eyes). Open eyes under the darkness. See the phosphenes\'97stars, colors, patterns generated by your own optic nerve.\
\
---\
\
## **Movement & Stillness**\
\
42. **Bed Cocoon Roll** \'96 Lie on the edge of your bed. Roll yourself tightly in the sheet/blanket like a burrito. Lie still, feeling the pressure on all sides. Slowly unroll.\
\
43. **Wall Sit with Book** \'96 Sit against a wall, thighs parallel to floor. Balance a book on your head. Focus on the weight and alignment. Hold until you tremble.\
\
44. **Floor Spread** \'96 Lie on the floor on your back. Extend arms and legs as wide as possible. Feel yourself occupying space. Wiggle fingers and toes at the edges of your reach.\
\
45. **Chair Float** \'96 Sit in a chair. Imagine the chair is dissolving. You are floating, held only by your own posture. Adjust micro-muscles to maintain the illusion.\
\
46. **Shadow Puppet Self** \'96 Make shadow puppets with your hands on a wall. Make them interact with your actual body shadow\'97puppet pets your head, puppet pulls your shadow hand.\
\
47. **Mirror Gaze** \'96 Stare at your own eyes in a mirror for 3 minutes without looking away. Notice when your face becomes unfamiliar, then familiar again.\
\
---\
\
## **Micro-Environment**\
\
48. **Fort Building** \'96 Drape a blanket over two chairs. Crawl inside with a pillow. Let the weight of the fabric create a sensory deprivation chamber for 10 minutes.\
\
49. **Closet Sanctuary** \'96 Clear a closet floor. Add pillow, blanket, book light. Sit inside with door cracked. Feel the containment of small spaces.\
\
50. **Bathroom Steam Sauna** \'96 Run the shower hot, close the door, sit on the floor outside the spray. Let the steam fill the room. Breathe the humidity until your skin dewdrops.\
\
51. **Car Nest** \'96 If you have a car, recline the passenger seat. Bring a pillow and blanket. Listen to rain on the roof or just the engine cooling. Safe pod.\
\
52. **Windowsill Perch** \'96 Clear a windowsill wide enough to sit. Add cushion. Sit with legs dangling inside, looking out. Threshold sitting\'97inside and outside simultaneously.\
\
---\
\
## **Evening Rituals**\
\
53. **Foot Oil Massage** \'96 Before bed, warm oil (coconut, sesame, or foot cream) between palms. Massage each toe, the arch, the heel. Pull each toe gently. Put on socks, sleep.\
\
54. **Wrist Cooling** \'96 Run cold water over your inner wrists for 60 seconds before bed. Lowers body temperature, signals sleep.\
\
55. **Sheet Spray** \'96 Mix water with a few drops of lavender or cedar in a spray bottle. Mist your pillowcase 10 minutes before sleep. Lie down into the damp coolness.\
\
---\
\
Pick the one that feels **impossible to do wrong**. That's your practice.\
\
\

\fs72 3 - FOR SOMEONE ELSE\
\

\fs24 \
   Here are **50+ small acts of human connection**\'97specific, doable today, real-world. Some are quiet, invisible kindnesses:\
\
---\
\
## **Invisible & Anonymous**\
\
1. **The Silent Return** \'96 Return a shopping cart for someone who just unloaded it, without making eye contact or waiting for thanks. Just do it and walk away.\
\
2. **Trash Walk** \'96 Pick up three pieces of litter on your block that aren't yours. No one sees you do it. The street is just cleaner tomorrow.\
\
3. **Elevator Button Pre-Press** \'96 If you're getting off before someone, press their floor button before they reach the panel. Don't mention it.\
\
4. **The Quiet Hold** \'96 Hold a door, then look away so the person doesn't feel pressured to rush or thank you. Let them pass through unobserved.\
\
5. **Parking Meter Extension** \'96 If you see a meter with time expiring, drop a quarter in. Leave no note. Never know if they noticed.\
\
6. **Library Book Treasure** \'96 Slip a handwritten note inside a library book before returning it: "You chose well. This one matters." Addressed to no one, found by someone.\
\
7. **Mailbox Lift** \'96 If a neighbor's mailbox flag is still up after dark, close it for them. Their mail is safe now. They won't know why.\
\
8. **The Dead Plant Removal** \'96 Throw away someone's obviously dead porch plant when they're not home. Replace it with nothing. Just remove the sadness.\
\
9. **Shopping List Completion** \'96 If you see a dropped shopping list in a grocery store, buy one item from it and leave it at customer service with the list. "Someone dropped this."\
\
10. **Windshield Wiper Check** \'96 Lift someone's wipers off their windshield if snow is coming. They'll never know who prepared them.\
\
---\
\
## **Micro-Interactions**\
\
11. **The Specific Compliment** \'96 Tell a stranger exactly what you like: "That color blue looks like the ocean on your skin tone." Then leave. No conversation.\
\
12. **Cashier Name Use** \'96 Read their name tag. Say it once, genuinely: "Thanks, Marcus." Watch them remember they have a name today.\
\
13. **The Slow Down** \'96 When someone is searching for words, don't fill the silence. Count to five in your head. Let them find their own sentence.\
\
14. **Bagging Help** \'96 At self-checkout, start bagging for the person next to you without asking. They'll protest. Say "I have time." Then stop when they seem uncomfortable.\
\
15. **The Headphone Removal** \'96 If someone asks you a question while wearing headphones, remove yours completely before answering. Show them the whole attention.\
\
16. **Stoop Sitting** \'96 Sit on your stoop at a consistent time. Wave to the same passersby until they wave back. Then just nod. Then just exist together.\
\
17. **Dog Treat Permission** \'96 Carry dog treats. Ask "Can I give your dog a treat?" before asking the dog's name. The human matters first.\
\
18. **The Photo Offer** \'96 See someone struggling to take a selfie or group photo? Offer to take it. Take three. Hand back phone. Walk away before they check them.\
\
19. **Heavy Door Wait** \'96 If someone's carrying something heavy, don't ask if they need help. Just open the door and stand there holding it. Let them choose to walk through.\
\
20. **The Crosswalk Slow** \'96 If a car stops for you at a crosswalk, walk slightly faster than necessary. Show them their kindness wasn't wasted on someone who didn't care.\
\
---\
\
## **Written & Left Behind**\
\
21. **Stranger Letter** \'96 Write a letter to a specific stranger you observed: "To the woman in the red coat on the 4 train who laughed at her own book." Don't deliver it. Burn it or keep it. The writing was the connection.\
\
22. **Bookmark Note** \'96 Leave a pressed flower or note in a used book at a bookstore: "I loved this part too." No signature.\
\
23. **The Coffee Shop Napkin** \'96 Write "You look like you're having a hard day. It's okay." Leave it on a table where someone just was. They might return. They might not.\
\
24. **Parking Note** \'96 If you see a car with an out-of-state plate in your neighborhood, leave a note: "Welcome. [Your city] is glad you're here." No contact info.\
\
25. **The Library Receipt** \'96 On the back of your library receipt, write a book recommendation for the next person who checks out that book. Leave it in the book drop.\
\
26. **Trash Can Art** \'96 Tape a small drawing or poem to a public trash can. "Even this is worthy of decoration." Signed, Anonymous.\
\
27. **The Bus Stop Bench** \'96 Leave a book you loved on a bench with a note: "Free. Finished. Yours now." Don't wait to see who takes it.\
\
28. **Mailbox Note** \'96 Mail a postcard to a random address in your city: "Hope your Tuesday was okay. \'97A neighbor." No return address.\
\
29. **The Receipt Poem** \'96 Write a haiku on the back of a receipt. Leave it in the shopping cart. Someone will find it in the parking lot.\
\
30. **Sticky Note Mirror** \'96 In a public restroom, leave a small note on the mirror edge: "You look like someone who tries hard. I see you."\
\
---\
\
## **Attentive Presence**\
\
31. **The Repeat Back** \'96 When someone tells you something mundane, repeat their exact words back to them later: "You said your azaleas are blooming early." They'll feel catalogued, cared for.\
\
32. **Birthday Calendar** \'96 Ask three coworkers or acquaintances their birthdays. Put them in your phone. Text them on the day with no gift, no card, just: "Glad you were born."\
\
33. **The Follow-Up Question** \'96 When someone mentions something small last week\'97a job interview, a sick cat\'97ask about it specifically today. "How was the interview? How's the cat?"\
\
34. **Name Remembering** \'96 Meet someone once. Use their name when you see them again in three weeks. They'll think you have a gift. You just have a note in your phone.\
\
35. **The Listening Lean** \'96 When someone is telling you something difficult, lean forward physically. Not to interrupt, just to reduce the distance between you.\
\
36. **Silence for Crying** \'96 If someone tears up in public, don't ask if they're okay. Hand them a tissue if you have one. Stand closer. Let them cry without explaining.\
\
37. **The Shared Gaze** \'96 If you see something beautiful\'97a sunset, a street musician\'97turn to the stranger next to you. Look at them. Look at it. Look back. Smile. Say nothing.\
\
38. **The Question Remembering** \'96 If someone asked you a question you couldn't answer, find the answer and tell them three days later. "You asked about that documentary. It's called..."\
\
---\
\
## **Practical Magic**\
\
39. **The Extra Umbrella** \'96 Carry two umbrellas. When it rains, hand one to someone without. "Keep it." Don't wait for return.\
\
40. **Phone Charger Loan** \'96 Carry a portable charger. Offer it to someone at 2% battery at a coffee shop. Let them use it while you sit across from them, not talking.\
\
41. **The Map Direction** \'96 If someone is clearly lost looking at their phone, offer directions without being asked. Then walk with them to the corner so they don't get lost again.\
\
42. **Hot Drink Handoff** \'96 If you're buying coffee and someone behind you looks like they're having a day, buy theirs too. Tell the cashier "It's anonymous." Leave before they know.\
\
43. **The Seat Surrender** \'96 On public transit, stand up and walk away from your seat without making eye contact with anyone who might need it. Let them take it without the performance of gratitude.\
\
44. **Luggage Help** \'96 If you see someone struggling with a bag on stairs, pick up the other end without asking. Carry it up. Set it down. Keep walking.\
\
45. **The Cart Return** \'96 In a parking lot, take someone's cart back for them after they load their car. They'll call out. Pretend you didn't hear.\
\
---\
\
## **Neighborly**\
\
46. **The Overhang Knock** \'96 If you share a wall or ceiling with someone, knock back once when you hear them. Just once. Acknowledge their existence without demanding conversation.\
\
47. **Shared Fruit** \'96 If you have a fruit tree, hang a bag of lemons or apples on a neighbor's doorknob with a note: "Too many. Please take." No name.\
\
48. **The Trash Day Move** \'96 If your neighbor forgets trash day, drag their bin to the curb. Drag it back empty. Never mention it.\
\
49. **Snow Shovel Extension** \'96 Shovel three feet of your neighbor's walk when you do yours. Just the three feet closest to you. Let them wonder if they did it and forgot.\
\
50. **The Light Check** \'96 If you notice a neighbor's porch light burned out, replace the bulb if you can reach it. Or leave a bulb on their step with a note: "For your porch. Matches your other one."\
\
---\
\
## **Digital & Remote**\
\
51. **The Voice Memo** \'96 Instead of texting a friend, send a 30-second voice note of you just breathing or saying their name twice. Let them hear your alive-ness.\
\
52. **Link Without Context** \'96 Send someone a link to a song or article with no explanation. Just the link. Let them discover why you sent it.\
\
53. **The Old Photo Send** \'96 Scroll back three years in your photos. Send a friend a picture of them you took that they don't have. "You looked happy here."\
\
54. **Goodnight Text** \'96 Text someone "Goodnight" who you don't usually text at night. Don't explain. Let them feel thought-of at the vulnerable hour.\
\
55. **The Review Write** \'96 Write a specific, detailed positive review for a small business you visited once. Name the employee if you remember. They might see it in a month. They'll know someone noticed.\
\
---\
\
Pick one that feels **slightly uncomfortable**. That's the one that matters.\
