\fs72 4 - Tech boundaries\
\

\fs24 \
  Here are **50+ concrete, practical, one-day tech boundary experiments**\'97specific enough to start in the next hour:\
\
---\
\
## **Phone Physicality**\
\
1. **The Drawer Morning** \'96 Put your phone in a drawer before bed. Don't open the drawer until you've showered, dressed, and had a beverage. Count the minutes between waking and touching it.\
\
2. **Grayscale Sunrise** \'96 Set your phone to grayscale mode for one full day. Notice which apps lose their appeal when drained of color psychology.\
\
3. **The Sock Pouch** \'96 Put your phone in a sock. The friction makes checking it an intentional act, not a reflex. Keep it in your bag, not pocket.\
\
4. **Airplane Mode Commute** \'96 Turn on airplane mode when you leave your door. Turn it off when you arrive. Notice what you observed during the dead zones.\
\
5. **The Back Pocket Ban** \'96 Keep your phone in your front pocket or bag all day. Notice how many times your hand reaches for the back pocket and finds nothing.\
\
6. **Flip Phone Simulation** \'96 If you have a smartphone, use only the phone app, texts, and maps for 24 hours. All other apps off-limits. No exceptions.\
\
7. **The Screen Down Rule** \'96 Every time you set your phone down, screen faces the surface. Picking it up requires an intentional flip. Count the flips.\
\
8. **Thumb Taping** \'96 Put a small piece of tape over your thumb's fingerprint sensor for one day. Unlocking becomes deliberate, not automatic.\
\
9. **The Far Reach** \'96 Keep your charger across the room from your bed. The 10-foot walk to check it at night is the boundary.\
\
10. **Notification Sound Swap** \'96 Change every notification sound to the same bland tone. Remove the dopamine-specific chimes that train your reward system.\
\
---\
\
## **App-Specific Limits**\
\
11. **Instagram Single Session** \'96 Allow yourself 10 minutes at 7 PM only. Scroll until the timer hits, then delete the app (reinstall tomorrow if needed). Notice the relief of not having it.\
\
12. **Email Three Times** \'96 Check email at 10 AM, 1 PM, 4 PM only. Set an auto-responder: "I check email three times daily for focus." Mean it.\
\
13. **Twitter/X Read-Only** \'96 Log in, scroll, but remove the ability to post for 24 hours (log out of posting account, use only a locked dummy account). Break the performative loop.\
\
14. **News After Noon** \'96 No news apps or sites before 12 PM. Your morning belongs to you, not the world's emergencies.\
\
15. **The YouTube Search Ban** \'96 Watch only what you intentionally searched for. No homepage, no recommendations, no autoplay. If you didn't search it, you don't watch it.\
\
16. **Reddit Front Page Only** \'96 Visit only the front page of one subreddit. No clicking into threads, no scrolling past page one. Surface only.\
\
17. **LinkedIn Sunday** \'96 Check LinkedIn only on Sundays. Realize nothing urgent happens there. Monday will be fine without your Sunday check.\
\
18. **WhatsApp Mute All** \'96 Mute every single chat for 24 hours. Check manually when you choose. Notice who actually needs you urgently versus who just sends memes.\
\
19. **The Amazon Cart Wait** \'96 Add to cart, but set a rule: no purchasing until 48 hours later. Today is browsing only. Tomorrow you can decide if you still want it.\
\
20. **Spotify No Algorithms** \'96 Listen only to full albums or your own playlists. No Discover Weekly, no Daily Mix, no Radio. Choose your own audio or let silence win.\
\
---\
\
## **Computer/Work Boundaries**\
\
21. **Single Tab Rule** \'96 Browser gets one tab at a time. Close it before opening the next. Feel the friction of your own distraction.\
\
22. **The 25-5 Hard Stop** \'96 Pomodoro technique, but with a twist: when the 25 minutes ends, you must stop working for 5 minutes even if you're "in flow." Break the addiction to momentum.\
\
23. **Slack Status Honesty** \'96 Set your status to "Focusing" or "Offline" for 2-hour blocks. Ignore all messages during. If it's urgent, they'll call. They won't call.\
\
24. **The Desktop Clear** \'96 Close every window, every tab, every document. Start from zero. Only open what you need for the next task. Close it when done.\
\
25. **No Multitask Monday** \'96 One screen only. If you need to reference something, write it down, then look. No split-screen, no dual monitors for a day.\
\
26. **The Download Folder Purge** \'96 Empty your Downloads folder completely. Set a rule: nothing stays there overnight. Deal with it or delete it.\
\
27. **Bookmark Bar Hide** \'96 Hide your bookmark bar for 24 hours. Type URLs manually. Remember what you actually visit versus what you click reflexively.\
\
28. **The Scroll Wheel Disable** \'96 Unplug your mouse, use only keyboard navigation for one work session. Feel how much scrolling is mindless.\
\
29. **PDF Print Rule** \'96 If you need to read a long PDF, print it or send to e-reader. No reading documents on the same screen you answer emails.\
\
30. **The Inbox Zero Lie** \'96 Declare email bankruptcy for the day. Archive everything older than 48 hours unread. If it mattered, they'll email again.\
\
---\
\
## **Evening & Night**\
\
31. **The Analog Alarm** \'96 Use a physical alarm clock. Keep phone in another room all night. Experience the first morning without checking weather in bed.\
\
32. **Blue Light Sunset** \'96 No screens after sunset without blue light glasses. Notice if you actually want the content or just the light.\
\
33. **The Kindle Only Night** \'96 After 9 PM, only e-ink devices allowed. No backlit screens. Your bed becomes a different kind of space.\
\
34. **TV Remote Hide** \'96 Unplug the TV or hide the remote in a drawer. Watching requires effort. Decide if the show is worth the walk.\
\
35. **The Dinner Phone Stack** \'96 Everyone at the table puts phone face-down in the center. First to touch theirs pays or does dishes. Even if dining alone, stack your own phone.\
\
36. **Night Mode Permanent** \'96 Set phone to permanent dark mode, lowest brightness, extra dim. Make the screen less appealing than the room around it.\
\
37. **The Podcast Walk** \'96 Only listen to podcasts while walking outside. No sitting and consuming. Pair audio with movement or silence.\
\
38. **Sleep Timer Mandate** \'96 Set a 30-minute sleep timer on any video or audio. When it cuts out, you don't restart it. You sleep.\
\
---\
\
## **Social Media Specifics**\
\
39. **The Story Fast** \'96 Post no stories for 24 hours. Break the documentation habit. Experience something without the meta-experience of sharing it.\
\
40. **Like No More** \'96 Scroll only, no liking, commenting, or posting for a day. Be a ghost. Notice how much energy liking actually takes.\
\
41. **The Follow Purge** \'96 Unfollow 50 accounts today. Anyone who makes you feel insufficient, anyone you don't recognize, anyone you scroll past. Curate ruthlessly.\
\
42. **DM Decline** \'96 Respond to no direct messages for 24 hours. Let them sit. Notice which ones actually require immediate response (none).\
\
43. **The Profile Visit Ban** \'96 Don't visit anyone's profile for a day. If you can't remember their handle, you don't need to see their content.\
\
44. **Hashtag Blackout** \'96 Search no hashtags, follow no trends. If you don't know why something is trending, you don't need to know today.\
\
45. **The Caption Delete** \'96 Write a post caption, then delete it. Post the photo with no text. Let it stand without your explanation.\
\
---\
\
## **Radical Experiments**\
\
46. **The Dumb Day** \'96 Use only a flip phone or no phone for 12 hours. Carry a notebook and pen. Meet people at agreed times like it's 1998.\
\
47. **Public Computer Only** \'96 Do all necessary digital tasks at a library or internet caf\'e9. The inconvenience is the boundary.\
\
48. **The Notification Zero** \'96 Turn off every single notification, badge, and banner for 24 hours. Check apps only when you remember they exist.\
\
49. **Screen Sabbath Sundown** \'96 Friday sundown to Saturday sundown, no screens at all. If you don't observe Sabbath, invent your own 24-hour sundown.\
\
50. **The Digital Will** \'96 Write down what apps/sites you'd use if you had only 1 hour of screen time today. Use only those. Ignore the rest.\
\
51. **Auto-Responder Realism** \'96 Set an auto-responder: "I am reducing screen time. I will respond within 48 hours. If urgent, call." Mean it. Keep it for a week.\
\
52. **The Scroll Count** \'96 Keep a tally of every time you open a feed-based app. Count them. Don't judge, just count. Awareness is the first boundary.\
\
53. **App Folder Hide** \'96 Put all social apps in a folder named "Later." Move the folder to screen 3. The swipe distance is the friction.\
\
54. **The Battery Anxiety** \'96 Let your phone die. Don't charge it until morning. Experience the freedom of being unreachable by accident.\
\
55. **Mirror App Check** \'96 Before opening any app, state your intention out loud: "I am opening Instagram to check my sister's post." If you can't say it, don't open it.\
\
---\
\
Pick one that feels **slightly inconvenient**. That's the one that will teach you something.\
\
